# Site performance, SEO, and Agentic Browsing prompt

Use this playbook when optimizing a site from PageSpeed Insights (WordPress, Vite/React, or similar). Detect the stack first and map each WordPress path to the equivalent (theme PHP → `index.html` / Vite entry; W3TC → `_headers` / `vercel.json`; plugins → npm dependencies).

## Phase 1: Diagnosis

1. Read PageSpeed Insights screenshot(s) or the live report and record:
   - Performance score
   - FCP, LCP, TBT, CLS, Speed Index
   - Accessibility, Best Practices, SEO
   - **Agentic Browsing score (n/3)** and every failed **AGENT ACCESSIBILITY** audit (copy the failing selector, snippet, and fix hint)
2. Inspect theme / app entry files:
   - `functions.php` or `index.html` / `main.jsx` (script/style enqueuing)
   - `header.php` or `index.html` (head tags, preloads, meta)
   - `front-page.php` / `index.php` or `App.jsx` / `Hero.jsx` (template structure)
   - `style.css` or global CSS (size and sections)
   - JS in `js/` or `src/`
3. Check active plugins (`wp-content/plugins/`) or `package.json` for conflicts (especially multiple cache plugins)
4. Check cache config (`wp-content/w3tc-config/master.php`, `public/_headers`, `vercel.json`)
5. Check `.htaccess` or host headers for caching/compression
6. Confirm `/llms.txt` exists as real Markdown (not the SPA HTML fallback)

## Phase 2: Critical Fixes (Highest Impact)

### A. Render-Blocking Resources
- Load Google Fonts asynchronously (`media="print"` onload trick)
- Add `font-display: swap` to all font URLs
- Preconnect to font origins in `<head>`

### B. LCP Element Optimization
- Identify the LCP element from PageSpeed Insights
- Add a preload tag for the LCP image in `<head>` (URL must match the `<img>`)
- LCP image: `fetchpriority="high"` and `loading="eager"`; do not start it at `opacity: 0`
- Move blocking scripts to defer or async

### C. Image Optimization
- Convert PNG/JPG to WebP with original fallbacks (`<picture>`)
- Serve sized assets (do not send 2000px images at 80px display)
- `loading="lazy"` and `decoding="async"` on below-the-fold images

### D. Unused CSS/JS
- Split or conditionally load page-specific CSS
- Defer non-critical JavaScript until after user interaction
- Keep WebGL / animation libraries off the critical path (lazy or idle)

## Phase 3: Caching & Delivery

### A. Browser Cache (W3TC or host headers)
- Enable CSS/JS cache control; lifetime 31536000 (1 year) for hashed assets
- Enable compression (gzip/brotli)

### B. Page Cache
- Enable page / HTML caching as appropriate for the stack
- Enable DB/object cache on WordPress; skip on static/Jamstack

### C. Minification
- Enable CSS and JS minification (or rely on the production bundler)

## Phase 4: Advanced Optimizations

### A. Third-Party Script Deferral
- Defer GTM / Analytics / similar until first input (scroll, click, keypress, pointer)
- Fallback timeout (8 seconds) for non-interactive users

### B. Critical CSS
- Inline above-the-fold CSS in `<head>`
- Include design tokens, layout, and hero styles

### C. Third-Party Audit
- List active plugins or npm dependencies and purpose
- Flag conflicts (e.g. two cache plugins)
- Recommend keep / update / remove; note known vulnerabilities

## Phase 5: SEO & Indexing

- Check `robots.txt` Allow/Disallow
- Verify search engines are not discouraged (WordPress Settings → Reading, or equivalent meta/robots)
- Canonical URL, sitemap (create or fix if missing/placeholder)
- Rank Math / Yoast (WordPress) or equivalent meta tags

## Phase 6: Agentic Browsing

Target: Agentic Browsing **3/3**. This is separate from Performance/SEO scores.

### A. Accessibility tree (required for agents)
- A well-formed accessibility tree is required for AI agents to interact with the page
- Failed audit to always check: **`<svg>` elements with an `img` role must have alternative text**
- Fix: `aria-label`, `<title>`, or `aria-labelledby` on every `role="img"` SVG (react-icons and Simple Icons often emit `role="img"` with no name)
- Decorative icons beside visible text: `aria-hidden="true"` (do not leave `role="img"` unnamed)
- Fix every selector the report lists, not only the first example

### B. llms.txt
- Serve Markdown at the site root: `/llms.txt`
- WordPress: web root or a plugin that writes `llms.txt`; Vite: `public/llms.txt`
- **Required:** first heading is an H1, e.g. `# Site or Project Name` (this is the only mandatory section per [llmstxt.org](https://llmstxt.org/))
- Recommended: blockquote summary, then H2 sections with markdown link lists (`- [Name](url): note`)
- If a catch-all SPA rewrite sends unknown paths to `index.html`, **exclude** `/llms.txt` (and `robots.txt` / `sitemap.xml`) so the audit does not score the homepage HTML as a malformed llms.txt (“File is missing a required H1 header”)
- After deploy, open `/llms.txt` and confirm it is Markdown, not the app shell

### C. Other agent checks
- Address any additional AGENT ACCESSIBILITY or WebMCP findings the report shows
- Do not trade away Performance/LCP fixes to pass these audits

## Output Format

For each fix, provide:
1. The exact file path to modify
2. The specific code change (before/after)
3. The expected impact (which metric improves and by how much)

After all changes, provide:
- Summary of all files modified/created
- Which files to upload or how to deploy
- Steps to clear caches and verify
- Expected score improvement (before → after) including:
  - Performance (and FCP/LCP/TBT/CLS/SI if known)
  - **Agentic Browsing (n/3 → 3/3)**

## Important Notes

- Only edit existing files when possible (create files when required: WebP, `llms.txt`, sitemap)
- Verify syntax; do not break JavaScript, animations, or WebGL
- Keep WebP with PNG/JPG fallbacks
- Test mobile and desktop viewports
- Re-run PageSpeed after deploy and confirm `/llms.txt` is not HTML
